(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-neuro-ophthalmology-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Neuro-Ophthalmology Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 40-year-old man has new upbeat nystagmus. How does the lecture classify this finding?","choices":["Normal physiologic gaze-evoked","Latent nystagmus","Spasmus nutans","Congenital and benign","Concerning and non-physiologic"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, and it warrants work-up in adults. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with a pupil-involved third nerve palsy asks why he is being sent for a scan tonight rather than in the morning. What is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["Scanning is only available at night","The pupil will not dilate tomorrow","An aneurysm could rupture within days","The palsy will become permanent by morning","Diabetes must be excluded tonight"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The threat of rupture within hours to days is what drives the urgency. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has a new visual field defect and a suspected chiasmal lesion. Which referral does the lecture list alongside ophthalmology?","choices":["Endocrinology","Rheumatology","Dermatology","Neurology or neurosurgery","Cardiology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with vascular surgery also named as appropriate. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has ptosis and miosis on the left with no anhidrosis. Does the absent anhidrosis exclude the diagnosis?","choices":["Yes, all three are required","Yes, unless the pupil is fixed","No, because anhidrosis is parasympathetic","Yes, unless the patient is diabetic","No, it depends on lesion level"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The triad may be incomplete depending where along the pathway the lesion sits. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with a long smoking history has a six-week history of right arm pain and now a right ptosis with a small right pupil. Which is the most likely underlying cause?","choices":["Brainstem stroke","Cluster headache","Thyroid carcinoma","Pancoast tumour","Carotid dissection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A superior sulcus tumour reaches the second-order neurons over the lung apex. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman develops new nystagmus with vertigo. After referral to ophthalmology, what does the lecture place next in the sequence?","choices":["Observation for six months","Initial imaging","Laboratory testing","Immediate patching","Corrective surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: referral, then imaging, then labs if relevant, to find the underlying cause. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with a large tonic pupil has normal eyelid position and full eye movements. Which finding argues against a third nerve palsy?","choices":["Photophobia","Blurred near vision","Unilateral involvement","Female sex in the thirties","No ptosis and no extraocular weakness"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The lecture makes that absence the discriminator. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a pupil-sparing third nerve palsy. How urgent is imaging, per the lecture?","choices":["Imaging only if symptoms persist beyond six months","Lumbar puncture before imaging","Imaging, but not stat","Stat angiography","No imaging at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Reassurance and imaging, without the emergency pathway. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has bilateral miosis with light-near dissociation. Which condition should be tested for first?","choices":["Giant cell arteritis","Tertiary syphilis","Multiple sclerosis","Diabetes mellitus","Neurosarcoidosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the classic association the lecture names. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has a left homonymous hemianopsia with macular sparing after a stroke. Which location does that pattern suggest?","choices":["Ciliary ganglion","Pretectal nucleus","Occipital cortex","Optic chiasm","Right optic nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Macular sparing is the cortical signature. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has binocular horizontal diplopia that worsens on looking to the left, and the left eye under-abducts. Which nerve is affected?","choices":["Left fourth","Left third","Left seventh","Left sixth","Right sixth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lateral rectus abducts, and the deficit is on the side of gaze. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has unilateral ptosis with reduced lid elevation, mydriasis and impaired adduction. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Adie tonic pupil","Bell palsy","Third nerve palsy","Horner syndrome","Myasthenia gravis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Reduced levator function with mydriasis and movement deficit. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman has new asymmetric nystagmus in one eye only. What does the lecture advise?","choices":["Refer for work-up","Reassure, it is physiologic","Repeat in one year","Patch the affected eye","Start a calcium channel blocker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Monocular or asymmetric nystagmus is specifically flagged as concerning. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A patient is discharged after naloxone reversal. Which point does the lecture emphasise?","choices":["Naloxone may need repeating","The pupils will stay dilated","Vision loss is permanent","Naloxone is intravenous only","Miosis is the dangerous feature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It lasts about two hours, so respiratory depression can return. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has a relative afferent pupillary defect on the left. Where does the lecture place the lesion?","choices":["Optic tract","Ciliary ganglion","Occipital cortex","Retina or optic nerve","Optic chiasm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and therefore pre-chiasmal. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has unilateral ptosis with normal levator function and ipsilateral miosis. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Adie tonic pupil","Argyll Robertson pupil","Horner syndrome","Third nerve palsy","Myasthenia gravis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Normal levator function with miosis distinguishes it from a third nerve palsy. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has an isolated atraumatic sixth nerve palsy and no known diabetes. Besides imaging, which test does the lecture add?","choices":["Lumbar puncture","Haemoglobin A1C","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate","Carotid Doppler","Tonometry"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, if he has risk factors and no established diagnosis. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man has a traumatic third nerve palsy. What does the lecture advise about corrective treatment?","choices":["Operate within two weeks","Start high-dose corticosteroids","Begin pilocarpine drops","Refer for laser iridotomy","Observe about six months first"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, with patching in the interim for binocular diplopia. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has lost the outer half of the visual field in each eye. Where is the lesion?","choices":["Optic chiasm","Right optic nerve","Left optic tract","Occipital cortex","Retina"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bitemporal hemianopsia is the chiasmal pattern. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has an isolated fourth nerve palsy with no history of trauma. Which cause does the lecture name as most common?","choices":["Aneurysm","Giant cell arteritis","Congenital","Microvascular disease","Intracranial tumour"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Isolated fourth nerve palsy is most commonly congenital, even in adults. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A patient shifts gaze from a distant sign to a book. Which three changes does the lecture describe?","choices":["Lens flattens, pupils dilate, eyes diverge","Lens thickens, pupils dilate, eyes converge","Lens flattens, pupils constrict, eyes converge","Only the lens changes shape","Lens thickens, pupils constrict, eyes converge"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The near response acts on lens, pupil and globes together. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A patient presents with a new neuro-ophthalmological complaint. Which sequence does the lecture summarise?","choices":["History and examination, then imaging if indicated","Imaging first, then history","Laboratory testing before examination","Immediate surgical referral","Observation for six months in all cases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with ophthalmology and appropriate specialist referral. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A parent asks about patching for a young child with a congenital palsy. What does the lecture specify?","choices":["It should be continuous until resolution","It is contraindicated in children","It should alternate hourly at home","It is unnecessary before school age","It should be supervised by ophthalmology"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, especially under about five years of age. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with hypertension and diabetes cannot abduct his right eye. Which cause is most likely?","choices":["Skull base fracture","Congenital palsy","Aneurysm","Microvascular disease","Intracranial tumour"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In adults that is the commonest cause of a sixth nerve palsy. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has vertical diplopia that worsens when she looks down and to the left, and tilting her head right helps. Which nerve is affected?","choices":["Left third","Left sixth","Right sixth","Left fourth","Right fourth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The head tilts away from the affected side to compensate. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has new involuntary rhythmic eye oscillations with a sensation that the room is moving. What is that symptom called?","choices":["Amaurosis","Oscillopsia","Vertigo","Diplopia","Photophobia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the sense of the environment moving, distinct from vertigo. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A patient has anisocoria that is greater in bright light. Which pupil is abnormal?","choices":["It cannot be determined","The larger pupil","The smaller pupil","Neither, this is physiologic","Both are equally abnormal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If it fails to constrict in light, the large one is at fault. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with headaches has lost the temporal half of the field in each eye. Which lesion location is most likely?","choices":["Optic chiasm","Left optic nerve","Right optic tract","Occipital cortex","Both retinas"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Crossing nasal fibres are affected, giving a bitemporal defect. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man develops left ptosis and miosis hours after a whiplash injury, with neck pain and left arm numbness. Which underlying cause should be excluded first?","choices":["Thyroid carcinoma","Carotid artery dissection","Pancoast tumour","Brainstem stroke","Cavernous sinus thrombosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A third-order Horner syndrome, and the reason this presentation is urgent. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has lost vision in the upper left quadrant of both eyes. What is this defect called?","choices":["Superior quadrantanopia","Bitemporal hemianopsia","Homonymous hemianopsia","Total monocular blindness","Central scotoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, a partial homonymous defect. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology STUDENT VERSION 2026.pptx, Slide 52"}]};
